{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur's tree coverage is beautiful — until it ends up in your gutters. The historic oaks in Albany and downtown, the pines throughout the Point Mallard area, the maples and sweetgums in established neighborhoods — they all shed constantly, and all that debris lands on your roof and slides into your gutters.</p>

<p>But Decatur has an extra challenge that most cities don't: river debris. The Tennessee River and Wheeler Lake create their own ecosystem of organic matter that goes airborne during storms and windy days. Leaves from miles away, seed pods, pollen, and fine particulates all settle on Decatur rooftops. It's not just your trees you're dealing with — it's everything the river brings.</p>

<p>In Decatur's humid climate, that debris doesn't just sit there — it decomposes into a thick, composted sludge that's harder to clear than dry leaves. The moisture accelerates breakdown, and within weeks of a gutter cleaning, you've got organic muck building up again. That standing moisture also accelerates corrosion on your gutters and provides a perfect breeding ground for mosquitoes.</p>

<p>Many Decatur homeowners find themselves in an endless cycle: clean gutters, wait a few months, clean again. At $150-200 per professional cleaning, that's $600-800 annually — which adds up to more than a quality gutter guard system would cost. Gutter guards break that cycle permanently.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are protective covers that fit over or inside your gutters, allowing water to flow through while blocking leaves, pine needles, twigs, and other debris. The technology varies by type, but the goal is the same: keep gutters clear without constant cleaning.</p>

<p>For Decatur's specific conditions — heavy tree coverage, river debris, and fine organic particles — we typically recommend micro-mesh guards. These use a fine screen (around 50 microns) that blocks even small debris while allowing water to flow freely. They handle the pine needles common near Point Mallard, the river-blown particulates that affect the whole city, and the larger leaves from oak and maple trees.</p>

<p>We also consider Decatur's humidity when recommending guards. Some guard designs can trap moisture underneath, accelerating fascia rot in humid climates. We recommend systems that allow airflow while still blocking debris — keeping your gutters and the wood behind them healthy in the river-town environment.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been installing gutter guards throughout Morgan County for over 20 years. We understand what the Tennessee River does to homes here — the debris patterns, the humidity effects, the unique challenges that come with living in a river city.</p>

<p>We're not here to sell you the most expensive option or push a one-size-fits-all solution. We assess your specific tree coverage, debris challenges, and existing gutter condition to recommend what actually makes sense for your Decatur home. Whether you're in historic Albany, near Point Mallard, or in Southwest Decatur, we'll design a solution that works for your property.</p>
